[[12860,4986,"During the course of the inquest the Welsh Ambulance Service Trust disclosed that in 2015 chest pains and related conditions were removed from the Red categorisation and placed in an Amber 1 categorisation whenever the patient is conscious and breathing","chest pains and related conditions removed from Red categorisation",""],[13511,5242,"There was a delay of over eight hours before an ambulance crew was able to convey Mr Kusz to the hospital","delay in ambulance response",""],[13512,5242,"The initial 999 call was made at 0822 hours and was then chased on at least seven different occasions by the care home and also his General Practitioner","delayed emergency call handling",""],[13513,5242,"The evidence revealed that the General Practitioner reported an occasional irregular heart beat and asked that an urgent ambulance was sent","inadequate clinical escalation",""],[13514,5242,"It was not until 1447 that a Clinician, employed by the Ambulance Service reviewed and undertook a secondary triage of Mr [name redacted] case","delayed clinical triage",""],[13515,5242,"One of the main factors accounting for the significant delay was the unavailability of resources/ambulances caused by extensive delays at hospitals across the region handing over patients at Accident and Emergency Departments","hospital handover delays",""],[13516,5242,"Delays of three to four hours were widely reported when the optimum period of time is fifteen minutes","excessive ambulance wait times",""],[13660,5314,"The delay in the attendance of an ambulance which, on the evidence, only arrived after police officers arrived on the scene and","delayed ambulance arrival",""]]